</figure>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">MDETR<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          ghttps://github.com/ashkamath/mdetr</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite> is a foundational vision-language model designed for grounded visual-language understanding, where it predicts object locations in conjunction with their corresponding textual descriptions. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Overview of Vision-Language Foundation Model ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a), the model employs dual encoders: a convolutional visual encoder (e.g., ResNet-101<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> or EfficientNet<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>) and a transformer-based text encoder (e.g., RoBERTa<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>). These encoders project both images and text into a shared latent space, where semantic alignment is enforced by maximizing similarity for matched pairs and minimizing it for mismatched ones. This joint representation allows MDETR to reason over spatial and semantic relationships across modalities, making it particularly effective for tasks such as phrase grounding, referring expression segmentation, and visual question answering (VQA).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">MDETR is pre-trained on a curated corpus of approximately 200k images encompassing around 1.3 million region–phrase pairs.These aligned pairs are sourced from diverse datasets including Flickr30k<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, MS-COCO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, and Visual Genome<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, ensuring dense grounding of objects to textual descriptions. The model employs a graph-coloring technique to consolidate multiple phrases per image, ensuring non-overlapping and semantically coherent region assignments that facilitate more effective representation learning. Trained at this scale, MDETR demonstrates strong zero-shot generalization capabilities across a variety of downstream tasks, including open-domain visual retrieval and fine-grained visual recognition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">GroundingDINO<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/IDEA-Research/GroundingDINO</span></span></span><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite> introduces a novel approach to open-set visual grounding, designed to localize and identify objects based on free-form textual descriptions. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Overview of Vision-Language Foundation Model ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b), the model builds upon a transformer-based architecture that integrates visual and linguistic modalities through a dedicated fusion module, enabling fine-grained cross-modal alignment between image regions and text queries. This design allows the model to effectively ground natural language phrases in images, making it highly suitable for tasks such as referring expression comprehension and open-vocabulary detection.The model is trained on a large-scale multi-source dataset comprising image-text pairs with bounding box annotations. Key data sources include Flickr30K Entities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, MS-COCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, Visual Genome <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>, and Objects365 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite>, which provide diverse and fine-grained grounding supervision across common and long-tail categories. This extensive pretraining enables robust representation learning for both visual and textual modalities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A major innovation of GroundingDINO lies in its integration of the DINO (Distilled Knowledge) framework into visual grounding. Through cross-modal knowledge distillation, the model captures fine-grained semantic information from textual descriptions and transfers this understanding to visual contexts with high precision. This mechanism enables GroundingDINO to localize objects in unseen images using only text prompts—without any task-specific retraining—showcasing remarkable zero-shot capability. This strength makes it particularly suitable for applications in dynamic visual environments and open-ended image retrieval tasks.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.10671v1/foundation_model.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/302;" width="476" height="302" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Popular image-textual foundation models developed in the community, which are widely used in the image-to-video transfer learning for addressing downstream video-language understanding problems.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">CLIP (Contrastive Language-Image Pre-training)<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/openai/CLIP</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> is an image-language foundational model that learns joint representations of images and text through contrastive pre-training. As Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Overview of Vision-Language Foundation Model ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(c) illustrate, its architecture consists of dual encoders: a vision encoder built on scalable neural networks such as vision transformers or convolutional architectures, and a text encoder based on transformer networks. These encoders are trained to map paired images and texts into a shared latent space, where semantic alignment is achieved by maximizing similarity scores for correct image-text pairs and minimizing them for incorrect ones. The model is pre-trained on a dataset of 400 million publicly available image-text pairs collected from the web, enabling broad semantic understanding. A key innovation of CLIP is its zero-shot generalization capability, enabling it to be transferrable to other perception tasks, such as image classification and cross-modal retrieval, without requiring task-specific fine-tuning. Empirical studies show that CLIP performs competitively with fully supervised models across 30 diverse vision benchmarks, particularly excelling in fine-grained recognition and open-domain retrieval.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:0pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(169.2pt,0.0pt) scale(51.7500789643539,51.7500789643539) ;"><span id="S2.F3.3.1" class="ltx_ERROR">{forest}</span>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Taxonomy of Image-to-video Transfer Learning Methods.
Existing methods are categorized according to the employed transfer learning mechanisms, forming two major groups: frozen features and modified features. The frozen-feature group includes approaches such as knowledge distillation, post-network tuning, and side-tuning, while the modified-feature group encompasses various fine-tuning strategies, including full fine-tuning, adapter-based tuning, LoRA, and prompt tuning. Each category is further divided into fine-grained and coarse-grained paradigms, reflecting different levels of temporal and semantic alignment between visual and textual modalities.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">BLIP<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/salesforce/BLIP</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> (Bootstrapping Language-Image Pre-training) is a unified vision-language framework designed to jointly optimize understanding and generation tasks through multimodal co-training. As Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Overview of Vision-Language Foundation Model ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(d) shows, its architecture integrates a vision encoder for image feature extraction, a text encoder-decoder for language processing, and a cross-modal transformer that dynamically fuses visual and textual representations. This hybrid design enables versatile applications, including visual question answering, image captioning, and text-guided image retrieval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">BLIP is effectively trained with a two-stage pre-training strategy. In the first stage, it employees a data bootstrapping mechanism to filter out noisy web-collected image-text pairs and generate high-quality synthetic captions via knowledge distillation. In the second stage, it optimizes the parameters by considering image-text contrastive learning to align modalities, image-text matching to verify cross-modal consistency, and generative tasks to produce contextually relevant textual outputs. Pre-trained on a combination of 129 million curated and 140 million web-sourced image-text pairs, BLIP achieves state-of-the-art results across 14 benchmarks, demonstrating strong robustness in low-data regimes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">BLIP-2<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/salesforce/LAVIS/tree/main/projects/blip2</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> further improves BLIP by additionally introducing a lightweight querying module to bridge pre-trained vision encoders and large language models, in order to align visual features with textual embeddings. This delivers significant accuracy gains on some downstream visual-textual understanding tasks like visual question answering, with introducing considerable computational overhead. BLIP and its extensions BLIP-2 have been widely used in the community for addressing multimodal reasoning tasks including healthcare diagnostics and interactive recommendation systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">LLaVA<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
            
            
            
          https://github.com/haotian-liu/LLaVA</span></span></span> (Large Language and Vision Assistant) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> is a vision-language model that integrates visual understanding with natural language processing through an end-to-end trainable architecture. As Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II Overview of Vision-Language Foundation Model ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(e) shows, The model consists of a pre-trained vision encoder CLIP-ViT-L/14 and a large language model (LLM) to process both textual and visual features. LLaVA employs a simple projection layer to align visual representations with the language model’s embedding space, enabling cross-modality fusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">The training of LLaVA is split into two stages to ensure better cross-modality alignment and task-specific adaptability. In the first stage (i.e., feature alignment pretraining), the projection layers in LLaVA are learned with freezing vision encoder, which aims to learn a good semantic alignment between visual and textual representations. The pretraining is conducted on a subset of CC3M (Conceptual Captions 3M)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib127" title="" class="ltx_ref">127</a>]</cite>, a dataset comprising 3 million image-text pairs. In the second stage (instruction fine-tuning), a large scale of instruction-following data generated by GPT-4, which includes approximately 158K multi-modal instruction-response pairs covering diverse tasks such as visual dialogue, detailed descriptions, and complex question answering are used to enhance the model’s reasoning and conversational capabilities. In this stage, both the projection layer and the LLM are updated and the vision encoder is fixed.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">LLaVA-1.5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib128" title="" class="ltx_ref">128</a>]</cite> extends LLaVA by replacing its image encoder with CLIP-ViT-L-336px and vision-language projection layer with MLPs, which achieve better multi-modality understanding. Moreover, the model also introduces a multi-patch partitioning strategy, which improves the efficiency of processing high-resolution images and facilitates dynamic-resolution inputs. It also extends the training data with academic benchmarks of varied vision tasks including visual question answer (e.g., OKVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib129" title="" class="ltx_ref">129</a>]</cite> and A-OKVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib130" title="" class="ltx_ref">130</a>]</cite>) and OCR-intensive tasks (e.g., OCRVQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib131" title="" class="ltx_ref">131</a>]</cite> and TextCaps <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib132" title="" class="ltx_ref">132</a>]</cite>). LLaVA-NeXT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib133" title="" class="ltx_ref">133</a>]</cite> improves LLaVA-1.5 by quadrupling input image resolution (up to 672×672) and supporting flexible aspect ratios inputs, enabling capturing finer visual detail. It enhances reasoning, OCR, and world knowledge through optimized visual instruction tuning while maintaining data efficiency with under 1M training samples. The model achieves superior performance across benchmarks, even surpassing Gemini Pro<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
            
            
            
          https://deepmind.google/models/gemini/pro/</span></span></span> in some tasks, while retaining LLaVA’s design and fast training scalability (e.g., 34B parameters trainable in nearly 1 day on 32 A100 GPUs).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion:</span> All these foundation models not only provide a solid foundation for improving image understanding in the presence of natural language but also set the stage for their application in more complex video analysis tasks, where the integration of dynamic temporal information becomes essential. Among these image-based foundation models, MDETR and GroundingDINO demonstrate stronger capabilities in capturing fine-grained visual–textual correspondences, as they are explicitly trained with grounding supervision to align object-level regions with textual phrases. By contrast, models such as CLIP and BLIP are more adept at handling abstract or high-level visual–textual correspondences, largely due to large-scale contrastive or generative pretraining. Instruction-tuned models like LLaVA bridge the two extremes by leveraging large language models to reason over both detailed and abstract alignments, though often at the expense of precision in object-level grounding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Overview of Image-to-Video Transfer Learning </span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we summarize common transfer paradigms for adapting image-level knowledge from Image-Language Foundation Models (ILFM) to address video-text learning tasks. Depending on whether the vision-language features extracted by ILFM remain unchanged or are adapted, we categorize existing transfer learning methods into frozen features and modified features, which will be elaborated in the following subsections.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Frozen Features</span></h3>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.10671v1/frozen_1010_roman.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/133;" width="476" height="133"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Methods and architectures of transferring pre-trained image-text model to video domain via frozen features.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By treating video as sequence of image frames in temporal order, many existing methods directly leverage pre-trained ILFM to extract frame-wise cross-modal features and then use the frozen frame-level features to facilitate their image-to-video transfer learning. Representative lines of work include knowledge distillation, post-network tuning, and side-tuning.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>1 </span>Knowledge distillation</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-A Frozen Features ‣ III Overview of Image-to-Video Transfer Learning ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(A), within the knowledge distillation strategy, ILFM is primarily employed as a teacher model to enhance the cross-modal alignment capabilities of image/text encoders in existing video-text understanding models. Various distillation objectives can be defined to facilitate targeted knowledge transfer across encoders, working in conjunction with the primary task loss. Although this paradigm has been widely adopted in early Referring Video Object Segmentation (RVOS) and Open-Vocabulary Multi-Object Tracking (OV-MOT) methods to introduce open-vocabulary capabilities from models like CLIP, it often overlooks the discrepancy between image-level and video-level representations. This oversight may introduce erroneous knowledge from ILFMs, consequently limiting the effectiveness of cross-modal video-text understanding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>2 </span>Post Network Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many methods achieve temporal modeling by additionally introducing a post network to process the frozen features obtained for each selected frame (shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-A Frozen Features ‣ III Overview of Image-to-Video Transfer Learning ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(B)). It is straightforward to construct the post network with temporal attention mechanism, enabling the information exchange among varied frames. However, this paradigm struggles to effectively capture dynamic temporal patterns from static features, resulting in limited performance. To more effectively harness the visual potential of frozen large-scale image models, researchers employ a Q-Former <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> architecture, utilizing a set of learnable queries to interact with features from all video frames. Through this approach, queries can dynamically capture temporal dependencies and motion patterns in videos, rather than augmenting image features with temporal relations. This not only reduces computational costs but also unlocks the potential of ILFM. Beyond general temporal modeling, many methods have incorporated more task-specific temporal modeling strategies, such as the Hungarian algorithm or Brownian bridge-based association. In summary, this paradigm relies on high-quality image-text features and carefully designed, task-specific temporal modeling architectures to achieve strong video understanding performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span>3 </span>Side-Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">as illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ III-A Frozen Features ‣ III Overview of Image-to-Video Transfer Learning ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(C), Side-tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> introduces a learnable side network alongside the frozen image and text encoders of ILFM. It receives the layer-wise latent features extracted by ILFM encoders as input, facilitating hierarchical spatial modeling and temporal refinement in a coarse-to-fine manner. In practice, the side network adopts a lightweight design, incorporating both text-modulated spatial pooling and temporal modeling operators <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>. During training, gradients are confined within the side-blocks, ensuring both parameter and memory efficiency. Recent study also show that the system can be further enhanced by connecting side-blocks with the outputs of CLIP, which achieves better results on several downstream video-text understanding tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion:</span> Frozen-feature-based image-to-video transfer learning frameworks offer notable advantages in adapting image-based foundation models to video–language understanding tasks. They maintain high parameter efficiency by freezing pretrained architecture and weights, thereby mitigating catastrophic forgetting of previously acquired knowledge. However, their performance may be limited by the inherent representational capacity of static image-level features and their imperfect alignment with video-specific task requirements, particularly when generalizing to novel scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Modified Features</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of directly using foundation models to extract frozen features, many approaches perform image-to-video transfer learning by explicitly adjusting the backbone model architecture and parameters, which we classify as modified features. In the following, we will elaborate on the techniques involved in modified features including fully-finetune, finetune with extra models, finetune with partial tuning, finetune with adapter, finetune with LoRA, and finetune with prompt tuning, whose architectures are presented in Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Fig. 5 ‣ III-B Modified Features ‣ III Overview of Image-to-Video Transfer Learning ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.10671v1/modified_1011.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/352;" width="476" height="352"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Methods and architectures of transferring pre-trained image-text model to video domain via modified features.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>1 </span>Full Fine-Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is straightforward to empower a pre-trained image-text foundation model with the ability of video-text understanding by building a temporal module on the top of each block of image-language foundation model and then simply finetuning all the parameters involved in the temporal modules together with the ILFM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite>. Although simple and effective, these approaches indeed are less applicable in practice as they need a large-scale well-defined and annotated data and a large amount of computing power in the model training stage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>2 </span>Partial Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Partial tuning strikes a balance between full fine-tuning and more parameter-efficient methods like adapters or prompt tuning. This strategy involves updating only a specific subset of the pre-trained ILFM’s parameters, while freezing the rest of the model parameters. Recently, several methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib134" title="" class="ltx_ref">134</a>, <a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>, <a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>]</cite> have introduced temporal attention modules into the original spatial attention modules, enabling ILFMs to acquire temporal–spatial modeling capability. Such tuning strategies strike a favorable balance between performance and computational cost, while still relying heavily on the inherent spatial capacity of the ILFM, as they do not involve full model training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>3 </span>Fine-Tuning with Extra Models</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Owing to the limited capacity of image-level features in capturing dynamic information, numerous image-to-video transfer learning methods incorporate auxiliary models to enhance temporal modeling. Currently, two dominant paradigms exist. The first paradigm augments image-based features with dynamic signals (e.g., optical flow, 3D spatio-temporal features) and introduces specialized modules to fuse or align them.
For example, in STVG, recent methods employ both image and video backbones (e.g., SlowFast<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>]</cite> or Video Swin Transformer<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite>), coupled with attention-based fusion modules, for collaborative spatio-temporal modeling. The second paradigm leverages the structural outputs of external models—such as detection results, segmentation masks, and motion saliency maps—to guide image-to-video transfer with lower computational cost. In summary, fine-tuning with extra models offers an effective strategy for injecting video-specific structural information into ILFMs. It is particularly suitable when video annotations are limited or noisy, and has demonstrated strong empirical performance across tasks such as referring video object segmentation, temporal grounding, and video-language retrieval.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>4 </span>Fine-Tuning with Adapter</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Adapter-based methods offer a parameter-efficient solution to adapting ILFMs for addressing video-language understanding tasks, which intends to insert a set of lightweight, trainable module (referred as adapters) into some intermediate layers of pre-trained ILFMs  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib139" title="" class="ltx_ref">139</a>, <a href="#bib.bib140" title="" class="ltx_ref">140</a>, <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite>. The adapter module are typically defined as a stack of temporal attention and cross-frame interaction operators in practice.
By keeping the parameters corresponding to ILFM frozen, adapter-based approaches can retain the ability of visual-semantic understanding gained in pre-trained ILFM while efficiently learn temporal patterns specific to video data through the newly introduced adapter. Recently, the adapter-based finetuning techniques are particularly attractive in scenarios with limited video annotations or computational budgets, and they are emerging as a prominent direction for addressing downstream tasks like video question answering, temporal video grounding, and video captioning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS5" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>5 </span>Fine-Tuning with LoRA</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As a representative parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) method, Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib141" title="" class="ltx_ref">141</a>]</cite> provides an alternative paradigm for ILFM adapting. Instead of directly learning the updating of model weights <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W</annotation></semantics></math>, LoRA select to approximate the updated weight with two small, trainable low-rank matrices, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta W=BA" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta W=BA</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times r}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A\in\mathbb{R}^{r\times k}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the rank <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\ll\min(d,k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≪</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\ll\min(d,k)</annotation></semantics></math>. During fine-tuning, the original parameters of the ILFM remain frozen and locked, preserving the extensive knowledge acquired during pre-training. Only the injected low-rank matrices <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are updated, which dramatically reduces the number of trainable parameters and the accompanying GPU memory footprint. This approach implicitly constrains the model’s exploration within a low-dimensional subspace, effectively mitigating the risk of overfitting, which is particularly beneficial when labeled video data is scarce. Consequently, LoRA has gained significant traction for efficiently adapting large-scale foundation models to various downstream video understanding scenarios, establishing itself as a cornerstone technique in resource-constrained environments. However, as LoRA itself is incapable of modeling temporal information, this paradigm relies on external temporal modeling modules or networks, such as those used in fully finetuning or post-network tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS6" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span>6 </span>Prompt Tuning</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prompt tuning has recently emerged as a highly parameter-efficient alternative for adapting ILFMs to video-related tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>, <a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite>. By freezing the backbone and introducing a small set of learnable prompts, the model is guided to capture temporal cues without modifying its core parameters. A common design is to share a single set of prompts across all frames to enforce temporal consistency, or to propagate prompts sequentially across frames so that information accumulates and evolves with motion. These temporal variants enable the frozen ILFM to move beyond static frame modeling and capture motion cues and cross-frame dependencies. This strategy achieves efficient spatio-temporal adaptation with low computational cost, though its reliance on frozen backbones may restrict fine-grained temporal reasoning compared to full or partial fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.SSS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Discussion:</span> The aforementioned image-to-video transfer learning methods primarily adapt existing image–text foundation models to address video–text understanding tasks through two main directions: enhancing the encoders of established video understanding frameworks or incorporating additional learnable temporal modules into image–language foundation models. Notably, these approaches are not mutually exclusive. Existing studies have shown that their integration can yield complementary benefits and further improve transfer performance. For example, fine-tuning with auxiliary models can be seamlessly combined with parameter-efficient strategies such as LoRA, facilitating the development of more advanced and robust video understanding models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fine-grained Video-Text Understanding</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we review methods that adapt existing pre-trained image-text foundation models to address specific video-text understanding tasks. Based on the required granularity of understanding, we categorize the video-text understanding tasks into fine-grained and coarse-grained classes, providing a clearer structural understanding and enabling researchers to identify methods most relevant to their research domains. We first introduce fine-grained video-text understanding, which requires precise localization of spatial regions (e.g., bounding boxes or segmentations), temporal intervals, or targeted editing of relevant video segments based on textual descriptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Object Tracking</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Open Vocabulary Multi-Object Tracking (OV-MOT) and Referring Multi-Object Tracking (RMOT) are two multi-object tracking tasks extended from traditional MOT, in which the objects of interest are explicitly referred with natural language descriptions. Specifically, OV-MOT aims to track all the objects <span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
            In this setting, the object number could be more than one and vary for different samples.</span></span></span>described by input text in an open-vocabulary manner, rather than relying on a predefined, closed set of categories. The performance of OV-MOT systems is measured through metrics including localization accuracy (LocA), association accuracy (AssocA), and classification accuracy (ClsA). In contrast, Referring Multi-Object Tracking (RMOT) takes a natural language description specifying the objects of interest as input, and aims to detect and track these objects within a given video. This task is challenging as it requires the model to jointly capture fine-grained visual appearance, motion patterns, and semantic language cues for maintaining persistent trajectories of referred entities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Most of existing OV-MOT methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>mainly follow a frozen feature paradigm to transfer useful knowledge from pre-trained CLIP and Grounding-DINO models. For instance, OVTrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> and OVTrack+ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> develop knowledge distillation blocks to enhance the RoI features extracted by ResNet50 and FPN with pre-trained CLIP, Based on OVTrack, VOVTrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite> further introduce object appearance self-consistency and spatial-appearance mutual-consistency losses to capture spatial relationships across frames, where object appearance/attribute information are extracted with CLIP. SLAck <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> also distills textual knowledge from CLIP into its semantic head and introduces a spatial-temporal object attention mechanism to jointly model semantic, location, and appearance cues. GLATrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite> extracts semantic information about the target objects from the reference frame using pre-trained CLIP model, which is then employed to reduce the influence of erroneous features in key frames. OVTR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> employs a post-network fine-tuning strategy, wherein a Transformer-based dual-branch decoder is introduced to process frozen features extracted by CLIP. These methods rely on coarse-grained image-text correspondences captured by CLIP, which restricts the model’s ability to capture the open-world objects with high dynamicity. MASA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite> introduces a plug-and-play tracking adapter to enhance open-world foundation models (e.g., Grounding-DINO) for tracking arbitrary detected objects. NetTrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> introduced a plug-and-play framework that integrates a grounding model (e.g., Grounding-DINO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib142" title="" class="ltx_ref">142</a>]</cite>) to establish fine-grained object-text correspondences and a point tracker module (e.g., CoTracker<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib143" title="" class="ltx_ref">143</a>]</cite>) to trace the trajectories of points of interest, thereby enhancing cross-frame object association performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Earlier RMOT approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib144" title="" class="ltx_ref">144</a>, <a href="#bib.bib145" title="" class="ltx_ref">145</a>, <a href="#bib.bib146" title="" class="ltx_ref">146</a>]</cite> mainly buit their systems based on a pretrained single-modal detection model (Deformable DETR), with cross-modal alignment and tracking modules integrated into this framework. Recently, iKUN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">88</a>]</cite> utilizes CLIP to encode the input query and each individual frame in video tubes, and subsequently employs a tracker to generate object trajectories. Furthermore, it introduces a knowledge unification module to guide the integration of visual features and textual information, producing text-specific visual representations and facilitating the selection of the most relevant tracking tubes. LaMOTer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite> conducts object tracking by detecting target objects in each frame with Grounding-DINO, predicting their locations in the next frame using an improved Kalman filter, and associating them to form the final tracks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Segmentation</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Open Vocabulary Video Instance Segmentation (OV-VIS) extends traditional VIS by enabling the segmentation and tracking of arbitrary object categories not seen during training, leveraging vision-language models to generalize beyond a fixed label set. In current research, the mainstream approach for this task leverages CLIP’s open-vocabulary capabilities by designing additional network modules appended after the CLIP encoder to achieve image-to-video transfer.
OV2Seg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> constitutes the inaugural work to incorporate CLIP into a post-network fine-tuning strategy for OVVIS. It employs a DETR-like architecture with object-centric queries to predict masks and perform cross-frame matching for tracking. The query embeddings, which fuse information from current and previous frames, are directly compared with CLIP-encoded text features to achieve open-vocabulary classification. InstFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">97</a>]</cite> introduces instance-guided attention into CLIP’s visual encoder, leveraging segmentation results as external cues to aggregate text-aligned instance features without compromising CLIP’s pretrained vision-language matching knowledge (using LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib141" title="" class="ltx_ref">141</a>]</cite>). Furthermore, it feeds the aggregated instance tokens into the tracking module to match instance tokens between previous frames and the current frame. BriVIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> employs a frozen video instance segmentor to generate frame-level instance queries and integrates multi-scale CLIP visual features for alternating short-term and long-term post-network temporal modeling. To model instance dynamics, BriVIS introduces a Brownian bridge to constrain instance features and adopts a contrastive objective, ensuring that the bridge center remains close to its corresponding class text embedding while distancing itself from irrelevant class texts. OVFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>]</cite> employs video-level instance queries, as opposed to image-level queries, and aligns video-instance queries with frozen CLIP features via a cross-attention mechanism to achieve unified segmentation and tracking across frames. OV-DVIS++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>]</cite> decomposes the task into segment-tracking-refinement stages, progressively modeling the problem from local to global contexts. CLIP-VIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> directly leverages CLIP’s image features to generate masks by combining predicted object scores and mask IoU. To alleviate the effect of historical noise, it first applies the Hungarian algorithm to identify the <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> most relevant frames from memory, and then performs instance tracking.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Referring Video Object Segmentation (RVOS) aims to segment and track a target object instance across video frames based on given natural language description, which typically specifies the object’s appearance, motion, and relational cues. Grounded-SAM-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">98</a>]</cite> and AL-Ref-SAM-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">99</a>]</cite> follow the framework of finetuning with extra model, which attempt to employ GroundingDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib142" title="" class="ltx_ref">142</a>]</cite> to detect objects in individual frames, then use the grounding results as prompts for independent segmentation foundation models (e.g., SAM-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib147" title="" class="ltx_ref">147</a>]</cite>) to produce high-quality object masks. ReferDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a>]</cite> proposes an end-to-end learning framework based on GroundingDINO, which first introduce a object-consistent temporal enhancer to capture the cross-frame object interaction. Then, it build a query pruning block on top of the pre-trained multimodality encoders in GroundingDINO, explicitly reducing the number of tokens for efficient inference. By combining the above modules and a mask decoder, ReferDINO succesfully empower the image-based foundation model with the ability of cross-modal temporal reasoning and segmentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temporal Video Grounding</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Temporal Video Grounding (TVG) is an important fine-grained multimodal video understanding task to localize the temporal boundaries of given language queries, which are typically in the form of single sentences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib148" title="" class="ltx_ref">148</a>, <a href="#bib.bib149" title="" class="ltx_ref">149</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, or even more complicated formats like paragraphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib150" title="" class="ltx_ref">150</a>, <a href="#bib.bib151" title="" class="ltx_ref">151</a>, <a href="#bib.bib152" title="" class="ltx_ref">152</a>, <a href="#bib.bib153" title="" class="ltx_ref">153</a>]</cite> or articles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib154" title="" class="ltx_ref">154</a>, <a href="#bib.bib155" title="" class="ltx_ref">155</a>]</cite>. Early approaches in TVG predominantly relied on a two-stage and proposal-based pipeline, where a large number of predefined candidate temporal segments are generated and then ranked according to their relevance to the language query. Afterwards, the field has shifted towards methods which are in a one-stage pipeline and those that can leverage powerful pre-trained image foundation models like CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> via image-to-video transfer learning. A pivotal development was the introduction of DETR-style frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib156" title="" class="ltx_ref">156</a>]</cite> that follow a Post-Network Tuning scheme. Moment-DETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite> pioneered this by reformulating TVG as a direct set-prediction problem, utilizing a trainable Transformer-based head over frozen backbone encoders. Building on this, BAM-DETR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite> introduced a boundary-oriented formulation and a dual-pathway decoder to achieve more precise temporal alignment. UniVTG<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite> proposed a unified multi-modal grounding head to handle multiple tasks within the same framework. UnLoc<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>]</cite> employed a post-pretraining scheme to fuse pyramid features from a CLIP model for both frames and text.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the post-network paradigm, other transfer strategies have been explored. VDI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> proposes a language-side adaptation paradigm for video-language models. While keeping the CLIP visual encoder frozen, it adapts the text encoder by infusing it with video-derived supervisory signals via a side-tuning approach. R<sup id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-Tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> introduced a Side-Tuning method in the form of a reversed recurrent tuning block, which performs query-modulated spatial pooling and coarse-to-fine temporal refinement. To incorporate explicit relational reasoning, PaTF<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>]</cite> encodes external cues by introducing a parallel transformer that processes structured scene graph representations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the advent of Multimodal Large Language Models has introduced a new research direction focused on enhancing their temporal awareness. LLaViLo<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">108</a>]</cite> was the first to utilize a video semantic modeling adapter to inject a joint feature representation into an LLM. To incorporate explicit time information as external cues, Chrono <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">96</a>]</cite> introduced a sequence-to-sequence approach that integrates video frames with their corresponding timestamp cues. Similarly, VTG-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite> integrated timestamp knowledge through sequence-time embeddings, absolute-time tokens, and slot-based token compression. ReVisionLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">109</a>]</cite> incorporated a recursive processing architecture with hierarchical adapters to progressively refine temporal segments in hour-long videos. In a training-free manner, TFVTG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>]</cite> employed LLMs to reason about sub-event relationships while using separate VLMs to localize each sub-event. NumPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">95</a>]</cite> employs a fine-tuning strategy augmented with explicit visual cues. It overlays unique numerical identifiers onto each video frame, thereby translating the temporal grounding task into a visual OCR problem that can be directly resolved by a Video LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spatio-Temporal Video Grounding</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spatio-Temporal Video Grounding aims to predict a spatio-temporal tube for the event depicted in given language query from an untrimmed video. HCSTVG-v1, HCSTVG-v2, VidSTG-Declarative, and VidSTG-Interrogative are the most popular benchmarking datasets used in the community. The evaluation metrics for this task include: (1) m_vIoU, vIoU@0.3, and vIoU@0.5, which measure the spatiotemporal localization accuracy; and (2) m_tIoU, which solely evaluates the temporal localization precision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Earlier methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib157" title="" class="ltx_ref">157</a>, <a href="#bib.bib158" title="" class="ltx_ref">158</a>, <a href="#bib.bib159" title="" class="ltx_ref">159</a>]</cite> use unimodal detectors like Faster R-CNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib160" title="" class="ltx_ref">160</a>]</cite> to detect objects in each video frame and then ground the temporal locations based on the detection features. STVGBert <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>]</cite> is the first work to introduce and fully finetune a pre-trained ViLBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib161" title="" class="ltx_ref">161</a>]</cite> model and develop a novel spatio-temporal composition/decomposition attention module, thereby enabling joint spatio-temporal modeling. Recent studies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>, <a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib162" title="" class="ltx_ref">162</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> have developed frameworks based on the powerful pre-trained MDETR model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib163" title="" class="ltx_ref">163</a>]</cite>, incorporating additional spatio-temporal interaction mechanisms. To achieve better performance, some approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> additionally introduce a temporal module to capture the cross-frame interaction and fine-tune all the parameters involved in the model. Specifically, TubeDETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> introduces a fast visual-only branch in the encoder and a time-aligned cross-attention mechanism in the decoder, enabling object queries to achieve joint spatio-temporal modeling. STCAT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a>]</cite> designs a video-level multimodal template in the decoder to maintain temporal consistency across spatial predictions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond adapting image backbones for dynamic information modeling, recent SOTA methods further incorporate video-level features from additional video backbones to enhance the capture of temporal dynamics.
CoSD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>]</cite> leverages SlowFast<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>]</cite> features and proposes a two-stream framework with cross-stream collaboration to capture static appearance and dynamic motion cues collaboratively for target localization. CoSTA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite> employs a static-dynamic encoder-decoder architecture, while additionally utilizing video features extracted by 3D-CNN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib164" title="" class="ltx_ref">164</a>]</cite>. During the decoding process, it entangles temporal queries with spatial features and bridges temporal
and spatial localizations. CGSTVG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>]</cite> and TA-STVG <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> integrate VidSwin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib138" title="" class="ltx_ref">138</a>]</cite> and MDETR to extract target-aware spatial and temporal cues under textual guidance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In weakly-supervised STVG, the recent SOTA methods have increasingly adopted post-network tuning strategies. The statical vision-language alignment knowledge learned by pre-trained grounding models (like Grounding-DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib142" title="" class="ltx_ref">142</a>]</cite>) is explicitly exploited in the community, alleviating the requirement of manual spatiotemporal annotations in the training stage. For example, CoSPaL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>]</cite> and STPro <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite> leverage pretrained Grounding-DINO to generate frame-wise spatial predictions, which are then concatenated to form a set of candidate tubelets with annotations. They then develop a curriculum learning strategy to capture spatio-temporal correspondences between sentence and tubelets, filtering out non-relevant proposals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Coarse-grained Video Understanding</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compared to fine-grained video-text understanding, coarse-grained tasks do not require precise temporal grounding results. They place greater emphasis on the holistic comprehension of events depicted in both text and video, such as determining whether a text-described event occurs in a video (Video Question Answering) or generating textual descriptions of video content (Video Captioning). In this section, we review methods for coarse-grained video-text understanding tasks from the perspective of transferring Image-Language Foundation Models to video domains.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-Text Retrieval</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video–Text Retrieval (VTR) aims to retrieve the most relevant video from a large corpus given a textual query, or vice versa. It serves as a fundamental task in video understanding and often acts as a precursor to more fine-grained video–language tasks. Since the introduction of the large-scale image–text pretrained model CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, a series of subsequent studies have focused on transferring its knowledge to video–text retrieval, achieving substantial performance gains over earlier methods. Early approaches in this domain typically adopt a full fine-tuning paradigm, where model parameters are initialized from pretrained weights and subsequently optimized for downstream tasks. that explored transferring CLIP’s image representations to the video domain for video–text retrieval. It employs a 2D or 3D linear projection layer on flattened patches and introduces an additional learnable token to extract frame-level embeddings, following a pipeline similar to that of the vanilla ViT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib165" title="" class="ltx_ref">165</a>]</cite>. In addition, a preliminary video-text post-pretraining approach was explored in CLIP4Clip to further adapt CLIP representations to the video domain. CLIP2Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>]</cite> extends image–text pretrained models to video–text retrieval by introducing a temporal difference block and a temporal alignment block. The temporal difference block enhances motion-related cues by modeling differences between adjacent frames, while the temporal alignment block aggregates text and frame embeddings through learnable center embeddings in the joint space. Another method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">80</a>]</cite> improves the full fine-tuning performance of CLIP for VTR by developing a weighted token-wise interaction mechanism and a channel de-correlation regularization loss. CLIP-Hitchhiker <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">78</a>]</cite> observed that most single-modal temporal modeling and aggregation strategies under-perform a simple average temporal pooling operation. To address this, it introduced a query-guided temporal aggregation baseline, which achieved performances superior to state-of-the-art methods. CenterCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">81</a>]</cite> proposes a more efficient full fine-tuning paradigm to transfer CLIP’s knowledge, where token clustering is conducted within each video segment and only center tokens are retained and further processed to compute the video-level embedding. TS2-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">115</a>]</cite> combines a temporal token shifting operation and a spatial token selection module to enhance the spatial-temporal video representation learning for better retrieval. CLIP2TV <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> constructs soft labels from the intra-modality similarity matrices to guide the learning of a fusion-based video-text matching module. X-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib124" title="" class="ltx_ref">124</a>]</cite> transfers the knowledge of pretrained CLIP models to end-to-end video–text retrieval through multi-grained contrastive learning across video–sentence and frame–word representations. In addition, an Attention over Similarity Matrix (AOSM) mechanism is employed to integrate similarity scores across multiple cross-modal levels. CLIP-ViP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib126" title="" class="ltx_ref">126</a>]</cite> introduces video proxy tokens as intermediaries for local–global information exchange, and further conducts video–text post-pretraining followed by downstream fine-tuning. To mitigate the injection of irrelevant visual information during video representation learning, X-pool <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">101</a>]</cite> introduces a text-conditioned frame embedding pooling module, enabling more effective transfer of CLIP for video–text retrieval. VOP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib125" title="" class="ltx_ref">125</a>]</cite> introduces a cooperative text–video prompt-tuning strategy to mitigate catastrophic forgetting of pretrained vision–language knowledge while substantially reducing the number of trainable parameters. Considering the mutual dependency between contrastive dual encoders and the spatio-temporal characteristics of video data, VOP inserts learnable prompt tokens at the inputs of each text and vision encoder block, with positional, contextual, and functional aspects of video prompts explicitly modeled to enable effective transfer. CLIPPING <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> proposes an efficient knowledge distillation framework with a novel Student-As-Base (SAB) scheme, which regards the student’s intermediate features as the bases of the teacher’s feature space to absorb the pretrained knowledge. PromptSwitch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib166" title="" class="ltx_ref">166</a>]</cite> inserts a cube tensor as learnable prompts into the CLIP image encoder, where the prompt cube can be flexibly transposed to assist in the peer-to-peer communication between each frame pairs in the video. RAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib167" title="" class="ltx_ref">167</a>]</cite> designs a low-rank modulation operation in the adapter module to inject the temporal sparsity and correlation characteristics for efficient image-to-video transfer. MV-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib168" title="" class="ltx_ref">168</a>]</cite> equips the adapter module with two novel components of temporal adaptation and cross modality typing that can effectively tackle the task of parameter-efficient video-text retrieval. TeachCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> introduces a multi-grained knowledge distillation framework that leverages a frozen CLIP model as the teacher. It jointly performs fine-grained frame-level distillation and coarse-grained video-level distillation, leading to strong performance in video–text retrieval. DiscoVLA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib119" title="" class="ltx_ref">119</a>]</cite> identifies three key image-to-video transfer gaps including vision, language and alignment and then introduces the IVFusion and PImgAlign to accordingly address the three transfer gaps for parameter-efficient video-text retrieval.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Action Recognition</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video action recognition is a classical task in video understanding, which aims to recognize the category of action being performed in trimmed video. Over the years, there have been plentiful research works making efforts to transfer the powerful pretrained image-text foundation models for tackling video action recognition. ActionCLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib121" title="" class="ltx_ref">121</a>]</cite> is a pioneering work that adopts a “pretrain, prompt, and fine-tune” paradigm for transferring the knowledge of CLIP, which formulates action recognition into a multimodal learning problem by extracting text representations of class labels for video-text matching. X-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">116</a>]</cite> introduces a new framework to expand existing large-scale image-text pretrained models for general video recognition, where a cross-frame communication attention module and a video-specific textual prompting strategy are incorporated for better transfer performance. EVL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>]</cite> proposes to utilize a sideway transformer decoder to progressively aggregate multiple intermediate features from a frozen pretrained backbone to a learnable query token that is regarded as the global feature for predicting action classes. ST-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib111" title="" class="ltx_ref">111</a>]</cite> presents an efficient spatio-temporal adapter which is instantiated as a 3D convolution module inserted at the front end of each pretrained block in cascade. Adaptformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">112</a>]</cite> designs another kind of adapter module which is inserted as a parallel learnable branch for merging into the original MLP’s output. AIM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">113</a>]</cite> jointly incorporates cascaded adapters into the spatial and temporal attention layers and adds an adapter in parallel to the MLP layer to achieve efficient transfer learning. BIKE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">106</a>]</cite> explores a bidirectional cross-modal knowledge transfer approach, where the attributes-category association and video concept spotting mechanisms are introduced to complement the unidirectional video-to-text matching and enhance the temporal awareness of video representations. ViFi-CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">79</a>]</cite> shows that simple full fine-tuning of pretrained CLIP models with late temporal pooling generalizes well to supervised settings and a vision-language prompting scheme works well in low-data regimes. Text4Vis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite> demonstrates that a new paradigm with frozen classifiers initialized from pretrained textual embeddings can achieve more efficient transfer learning as compared to previous contrastive-based paradigms. DiST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>]</cite> disentangles spatial and temporal modeling to the pretrained backbone and a learnable network, and further utilizes a side branch to integrate multiple intermediate features from the spatial and temporal streams. UniFormerV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> designs a sophisticated transfer method by additionally learning local temporal modeling and global spatio-temporal modeling on top of the pretrained global spatial modeling and a multi-stage fusion block is utilized to aggregate video tokens from different semantic levels. DUALPATH <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">114</a>]</cite> explicitly separates the image-to-video adaptation into a spatial path with parallel adapters and a temporal path with serial adapters followed by a late path fusion for action classification. In addition, a new manner to imitate spatial modeling to achieve efficient temporal modeling via token interactions in a grid-like frameset is also proposed. MoMa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>]</cite> proposes a new temporal modeling method called sequence modulation operation, which is achieved by efficient trainable mamba layers to inject fine-grained spatio-temporal information into intermediate features. D<sup id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>ST <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">117</a>]</cite> develops a dual-pathway adapter that enables disentangled adaptation of spatial and temporal features, while maintaining full compatibility with pretrained single-stream image-text models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Captioning</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Video captioning aims to generate natural language descriptions of video content. It has evolved from early template-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib169" title="" class="ltx_ref">169</a>, <a href="#bib.bib170" title="" class="ltx_ref">170</a>, <a href="#bib.bib171" title="" class="ltx_ref">171</a>]</cite> to the adaptation of large pre-trained foundation models. Many works adopt a post-network tuning strategy to enhance features from frozen image encoders. Clip4Caption <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> introduces a post-network for temporal segment sampling and utilizes contrastive pretraining to learn text-aligned video embeddings from CLIP features. EvCap <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">105</a>]</cite> also employs a post-network approach to fuse global visual contexts with specific action and object cues extracted by a pretrained CLIP model. mPLUG-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">87</a>]</cite> introduces a partial pretraining paradigm where a dual-vision encoder decouples spatial and temporal modeling by augmenting shared spatial self-attention layers with a novel local temporal modeling module for video inputs. MAMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> introduces a model-agnostic Side-Tuning framework that utilizes a module selector to adaptively choose between captioning modules of different sizes and incorporates an adaptive attention masking scheme to focus on important visual tokens. Similarly, CM<sup id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">104</a>]</cite> enhances CLIP features using multi-scale temporal convolutions and a cross-modal memory module that retrieves relevant textual semantics as external cues. In contrast, SWINBERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a>]</cite> proposes an end-to-end fine-tunable architecture, employing a Video Swin Transformer to extract dense spatio-temporal tokens which are then processed by a multimodal transformer with sparse attention for effective long-range modeling. More recent approaches focus on improving temporal precision. Vid2Seq <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">103</a>]</cite> presents a unified framework for joint event captioning and localization by augmenting the language model’s vocabulary with special time tokens that serve as explicit temporal models. VTG-LLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">100</a>]</cite> enhances pretrained encoders by injecting temporal cues like sequential and absolute time embeddings and employs a slot-based token compression mechanism for the efficient handling of long video sequences. HierarQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> leverages a trainable hierarchical Q-Former as post network, equipped with a two-stream feature modulator and dedicated memory banks, to compress frozen video features into a compact representation for a LoRA-adapted LLM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Question Answering</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pioneering methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib172" title="" class="ltx_ref">172</a>, <a href="#bib.bib173" title="" class="ltx_ref">173</a>, <a href="#bib.bib174" title="" class="ltx_ref">174</a>, <a href="#bib.bib175" title="" class="ltx_ref">175</a>]</cite> either apply hierarchical spatio-temporal modeling strategies, employing two-stage architectures to first extract key frame clips followed by reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib176" title="" class="ltx_ref">176</a>]</cite>, or constructing spatio-temporal relational graphs via graph neural networks. Recently, more and more methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>, <a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite> consider addressing the VideoQA problem utilizing the pre-trained foundation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Many studies have focused on exploring frozen-feature strategies, such as knowledge distillation and post-network tuning, for image-to-video transfer learning. VideoDistill <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> introduces a language-aware gating module that dynamically selects and refines frame-level visual features. This design facilitates goal-oriented selective knowledge distillation and effectively mitigates language bias that often arises when adapting image-based models to video data.
BIMBA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> adopts the post-network tuning strategy by introducing a Mamba-based token compression module on top of pre-trained foundation models. The module models long-range spatiotemporal dependencies and compresses redundant tokens, thereby improving efficiency. LinVT<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> implements post-network tuning through a plug-and-play Linear Video Tokenizer module. Placed after a frozen visual encoder, this post-network employs a Spatio-Temporal Visual Token Refiner (SVR) and a Text-Conditioned Token Aggregator (TTA) to produce a compact, question-relevant video representation for the subsequent large language model. HierarQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite> develops a post-network tuning module in the form of a trainable hierarchical Q-Former equipped with task-aware modulators and memory mechanisms. It generates a compact yet informative spatiotemporal representation, which is subsequently fed into a frozen large language model that is further adapted through a lightweight adapter for textual answer generation. TEA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">86</a>]</cite> extends TextVQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib177" title="" class="ltx_ref">177</a>]</cite> from images to videos by introducing spatio-temporal recovery modules into a pretrained T5 language model. These modules, consisting of a temporal convolution block and an OCR-enhanced relative spatial bias, can capture spatio-temporal clues to support video question answering.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recent progress in Multimodal Large Language Models has established a new paradigm for Video QA, heavily reliant on the integration of modified visual and textual features. Atemporal Probe (ATP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib120" title="" class="ltx_ref">120</a>]</cite> introduces a fine-tuning with prompt tuning approach that selects a single representative key frame to serve as a learnable visual prompt for a frozen vision encoder. Q-ViD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite> leverages a pre-trained InstructBLIP model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib178" title="" class="ltx_ref">178</a>]</cite> and incorporates tailored instructional prompts to generate descriptive video captions, using them as contextual cues for improved question answering. Similarly, ViTiS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib122" title="" class="ltx_ref">122</a>]</cite> employs multimodal prompt learning together with a visual mapping network to inject task-specific cues into image-language models, enabling strong performance with only few additional learnable parameters and limited training data. Other works explore adapter-based fine-tuning. Tem-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">110</a>]</cite> introduces a visual temporal aligner to predict future video states and a textual semantic aligner to refine embeddings for cross-modal alignment. TR-Adapter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib118" title="" class="ltx_ref">118</a>]</cite> proposes a temporal reasoning adapter designed to capture temporal relationships and facilitate knowledge transfer from language models. Unlike prior methods that maintain the original backbone architecture and perform adaptation through external lightweight modules, TC-LLaVA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">84</a>]</cite> modifies the attention computation in foundation models through partial tuning. Specifically, it introduces a Temporal-Aware Dual RoPE to encode temporal positional information and a Frame-wise Block Causal Attention Mask to enhance interactions among visual tokens.</p>
</div>
