</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Analysis</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following, we present the experimental results of various transfer learning techniques applied to different video-text understanding tasks, followed by a detailed analysis.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temporal Video Grounding</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span>
Temporal Video Grounding (TVG) aims to locate segments (or moment) in given untrimmed video based on a descriptive sentence. To address the TVG problem, numerous datasets have been constructed with video–text pairs and detailed annotations specifying the temporal spans of events described in the text. Specifically, DiDeMo<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib149" title="" class="ltx_ref">149</a>]</cite>, TACoS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib180" title="" class="ltx_ref">180</a>]</cite>, Charades-STA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib148" title="" class="ltx_ref">148</a>]</cite> and ActivityNet Captions<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib181" title="" class="ltx_ref">181</a>]</cite> are three most widely used benchmarks in the community. Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib148" title="" class="ltx_ref">148</a>]</cite>, most existing approaches are evaluated in these datasets and report their performance in the term of metrics ”Rn@m”and ”mIoU”. Metric ”Rn@m” is employed to compute the the percentage of samples where the top-<math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> predicted segments has an IoU greater than a predefined threshold <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is typically defined as <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Metric ”mIoU” calculates the mean IoU between the predicted segments and ground truth on all testing data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and Analysis.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ V-E General Video Understanding ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, results for Temporal Video Grounding (TVG) reveal a notable performance gap between specialized architectures built on CLIP and generalist Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) adapted from LLaVA. Among CLIP-based models, the Side-Tuning method R<sup id="S6.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>-tuning achieves the highest precision with 59.8% on R@0.5 and 50.9% on m_I0U. This can be attributed to its ability to refine multi-level visual features within a dedicated temporal modeling module. In contrast, for MLLM-based methods, NumPro leads with 42.0% on R@0.5 and 41.4% on m_IoU by employing a Fine-Tuning with Extra Models strategy that reformulates temporal regression into a visual OCR task, better aligning with the MLLM’s native strengths. The observed results suggest that for TVG—a task requiring precise temporal regression—specialized, decoupled architectures currently hold an advantage over temporally-augmented general-purpose MLLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video-Text Retrieval</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span>
Video Text Retrieval (VTR) aims to identify relevant videos from a large collection based on a given descriptive sentence, or vice versa. To facilitate research in this area, several datasets have been constructed, pairing videos with textual descriptions and enabling the evaluation of cross-modal alignment performance. Among these, MSR-VTT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib182" title="" class="ltx_ref">182</a>]</cite> is one of the most widely used benchmarks in the field. Following common practice, most existing approaches are evaluated on this dataset and report performance using the metrics R@1, R@5, R@10, and R@sum. These metrics are defined as follows: R1, R5, and R10 measure the recall rate at the top 1, 5, and 10 retrieved results, respectively, indicating the model’s ability to rank relevant items at the top. Rsum is the sum of R1, R5, and R10, providing an aggregated measure of retrieval performance across different ranks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> By examining the results presented in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Video Question Answering ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> for the task of Video-Text Retrieval, it can be observed that most recent methods are built upon the pretrained CLIP model due to its strong visual-textual correspondence capability. Among these, fine-tuning with LoRA achieves the best performance, significantly surpassing other feature-modification transfer learning strategies such as prompt tuning, knowledge distillation, and adapter-based tuning, in the metric of R@1. As expected, directly fusing CLIP-extracted features with a post-network tuning scheme performs worse than most of the approaches that updates CLIP parameters, highlighting the importance of adapting the original foundation model to the target task and dataset for optimal performance.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Action Recognition</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span>
Video Action Recognition (VAR) is a classical task in video understanding, which aims to recognize the category of human action being performed in a trimmed video. To support research in this area, several influential datasets have been developed. Foundational benchmarks include UCF101 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib183" title="" class="ltx_ref">183</a>]</cite> and HMDB51 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib184" title="" class="ltx_ref">184</a>]</cite>, which established early standards for the task. More recently, the large-scale Kinetics series(e.g., Kinetics-400 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib185" title="" class="ltx_ref">185</a>]</cite>, Kinetics-600 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib186" title="" class="ltx_ref">186</a>]</cite>, and Kinetics-700 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib187" title="" class="ltx_ref">187</a>]</cite>) has become the most widely adopted benchmark for evaluating modern, high-capacity models. Following common practice, model performance on these datasets is primarily evaluated using Top-1 and Top-5 accuracy. Top-1 accuracy measures the percentage of videos for which the model’s single highest-probability prediction is correct. Top-5 accuracy measures the percentage of videos where the ground-truth label is among the model’s five most confident predictions, which is particularly informative for datasets with a large number of classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Video Question Answering ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> reports a detailed comparison of several representative image-to-video transfer methods for video action recognition based on the CLIP model. Overall, finetuning with Adapter strategy achieves better results than side-tuning strategy in this task. MoMa <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">102</a>]</cite> obtains the best results on both K400 and SSv2 sets, which employs a mamba architecture to learn long-term fine-grained spatio-temporal information. The comparison between EVL and DiST demonstrates the effectiveness of disentangling the spatial and temporal modeling in image-to-video transfer learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Question Answering</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span> Video Question Answering (VideoQA) task is typically evaluated using metrics such as Top-1 Accuracy, Top-K Accuracy, and Average Normalized Levenshtein Similarity (ANLS)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib188" title="" class="ltx_ref">188</a>]</cite>, which measure the alignment between the predicted answers and ground truth answers. The VideoQA task is often subdivided into three distinct subcategories based on the nature of the questions and the expected answers: <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">i):Multiple-Choice (MC):</span> This subcategory involves answering questions that have a predefined set of possible answers. Common benchmarks for evaluating MC-based VideoQA tasks include TGIF-Action and TGIF-Transition<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib172" title="" class="ltx_ref">172</a>]</cite>, which focus on action-based queries within video clips, and MSRVTT-MC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib182" title="" class="ltx_ref">182</a>]</cite> and LSMDC-MC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib189" title="" class="ltx_ref">189</a>]</cite>, which extend this challenge to larger, more diverse video datasets. <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">ii):Open-Ended (OE):</span> In this subcategory, the questions posed are more open-ended, allowing for subjective, creative, and sometimes logical responses. Popular benchmarks in the OE VideoQA subtask include TGIF-Frame <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib172" title="" class="ltx_ref">172</a>]</cite>, which deals with frame-level visual understanding; MSRVTT-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib182" title="" class="ltx_ref">182</a>]</cite>, which presents questions based on the MSR-VTT video dataset; MSVD-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib190" title="" class="ltx_ref">190</a>]</cite>, which uses the MSVD dataset for question answering; and LSMDC-FiB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib189" title="" class="ltx_ref">189</a>]</cite> and ActivityNet-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib191" title="" class="ltx_ref">191</a>]</cite>, which extend the challenge to more diverse and dynamic video sequences. <span id="S6.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">iii):Long-Form (LF):</span> The Long-Form VideoQA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib192" title="" class="ltx_ref">192</a>]</cite> task takes VideoQA a step further by not only requiring a single answer but also necessitating the generation of comprehensive, detailed explanations. Key benchmarks in this subtask include ActivityNet-QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib191" title="" class="ltx_ref">191</a>]</cite>, How2QA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib193" title="" class="ltx_ref">193</a>]</cite>, and VIOLIN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib194" title="" class="ltx_ref">194</a>]</cite>, all of which emphasize the generation of detailed, explanatory answers that require a deep understanding of video context and reasoning across time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> We present the comparison results on four datasets in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Video Question Answering ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, where most competitors use CLIP as their backbone to ensure a fair comparison. For standard vision-language models such as CLIP, various transfer methods demonstrate strong and dataset-dependent performance. The distillation-based approach, VideoDistill <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, achieves state-of-the-art results on MSRVTT-MC and MSVD-QA with accuracies of 97.8% and 49.2%, respectively. In contrast, on the How2QA benchmark, the prompt-tuning method Q-Vid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib123" title="" class="ltx_ref">123</a>]</cite> attains the highest score at 71.4%. These results suggest that for a fixed backbone like CLIP, the most effective adaptation strategy varies across different benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely,the integration of Large Language Models as the foundation model substantially elevates performance. On the NextQA benchmark, the LLaVA-based BIMBA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite> model, which utilizes a post-network tuning strategy, achieves an accuracy of 83.73%. This represents a significant margin of improvement over the best CLIP-based method, Q-Vid with an accuracy of 66.3% on the same dataset. This finding underscores that post-network tuning is a potent transfer strategy for LLM-based models, as it efficiently adapts visual information to unlock the LLM’s powerful, built-in semantic reasoning for video understanding.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-E</span> </span><span id="S6.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Video Captioning</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span> A variety of standard benchmarks are collected to train and evaluate video captioning models. Prominent open-domain datasets include MSRVTT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib195" title="" class="ltx_ref">195</a>]</cite>, comprising 10,000 video clips across 20 categories, and MSVD dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib196" title="" class="ltx_ref">196</a>]</cite>. Both datasets provide multiple human-annotated reference captions per video, making them ideal for evaluating models’ general-purpose descriptive capabilities and serving as foundational benchmarks for assessing performance across diverse scenarios. For caption tasks that require specialized procedural understanding, datasets such as YouCook2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib197" title="" class="ltx_ref">197</a>]</cite> are commonly used. This dataset contains long instructional videos with temporally segmented annotations, challenging models to generate fine-grained, step-by-step descriptions of procedural content.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The performance of video captioning models is typically evaluated by comparing machine-generated candidate captions with a set of human-written reference captions using a suite of automated metrics. These metrics primarily measure both lexical similarity and semantic alignment between the generated and reference captions. For instance, BLEU@N <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib198" title="" class="ltx_ref">198</a>]</cite> is a precision-oriented metric that measures n-gram overlap to evaluate fluency, while the recall-oriented ROUGE-L <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib199" title="" class="ltx_ref">199</a>]</cite> uses the Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) to assess content fidelity. Building on these, METEOR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib200" title="" class="ltx_ref">200</a>]</cite> achieves better agreement with human judgment by considering synonyms and word stems, providing a more semantically informed evaluation of generated captions. CIDEr <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib201" title="" class="ltx_ref">201</a>]</cite>, developed specifically for captioning, measures consensus by representing sentences as TF-IDF–weighted n-gram vectors, rewarding captions that capture the salient information shared across the reference set. These metrics provide a holistic evaluation of caption quality, capturing both linguistic accuracy and semantic fidelity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ V-D Video Question Answering ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>, recent advancements in video captioning are driven by modified transfer strategies that outperform traditional fine-tuning. The side-tuning method MAMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>]</cite> achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple key metrics (60.0 BLEU4, 71.2 ROUGE-L, 82.9 CIDEr). This success can be primarily attributed to its architectural design; by training a separate, lightweight module while keeping the foundation model frozen, it effectively learns task-specific temporal dynamics without the risk of catastrophic forgetting. The strong performance of the LLaVA-based HierarQ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, which excels by aligning visual features with an LLM’s powerful reasoning, further validates this trend. These results indicate that the most effective transfer strategies are those that intelligently integrate new knowledge while preserving the core capabilities of the pre-trained backbone.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">VI-F</span> </span><span id="S6.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Spatio-Temporal Video Grounding</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span> For the STVG task, VidSTG is currently the most widely-used benchmark dataset. It contains 99,943 video-text
pairs, including 44,808 declarative sentences and 55,135 interrogative sentences from 10,303 videos spanning 80
object categories. For clearer comparison, we evaluate different methods under the declarative sentences
setting. As for metrics, we follow previous works<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> to use mean vIoU as
the main metric. vIoU is computed as: <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="vIoU=\frac{1}{\left|T_{u}\right|}\sum_{t\in T_{i}}IoU(\hat{b}_{t},b_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">vIoU=\frac{1}{\left|T_{u}\right|}\sum_{t\in T_{i}}IoU(\hat{b}_{t},b_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math>
where <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{u}</annotation></semantics></math> indicate the intersection and union between the time intervals obtained from annotation and prediction, respectively. <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{b}_{t},b_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>b</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{b}_{t},b_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are the predicted and ground truth bounding box for the <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-th frame, respectively. We average the vIoU score over all the samples to obtain mean vIoU (termed m_vIoU). We also report vIoU@R which indicates the proportion of samples with vIoU higher than <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. We also follow TubeDETR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> and report the tIoU evaluating the temporal grounding performances. It is defined as: <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tIoU=\frac{\left|T_{i}\right|}{\left|T_{u}\right|}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tIoU=\frac{\left|T_{i}\right|}{\left|T_{u}\right|},</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{gt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{gt}</annotation></semantics></math> indicates the set of frames inside the ground truth time interval.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> In fully-supervised STVG tasks, nearly all existing approaches adopt MDETR<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib163" title="" class="ltx_ref">163</a>]</cite> as the foundation model, as it provides essential visual grounding capabilities and can be fully adapted to video-level understanding through fine-tuning. However, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ V-E General Video Understanding ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, performing temporal modeling solely on the image-level features extracted by MDETR leads to limited temporal localization accuracy. Consequently, recent methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">89</a>, <a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>, <a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">91</a>, <a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> have incorporated auxiliary models to extract video-level features, such as SlowFast<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib137" title="" class="ltx_ref">137</a>]</cite> and 3D-CNN<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib164" title="" class="ltx_ref">164</a>]</cite>, which substantially improve temporal localization performance, as evidenced by the increase in tIoU from 48.1 in TubeDETR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>]</cite> to 52.1 in CoSTA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">90</a>]</cite>. These results suggest that integrating both image-level and video-level features enables the model to achieve more precise fine-grained spatio-temporal localization. However, incorporating auxiliary models also increases computational cost; for example, TASTVG<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">92</a>]</cite> was trained on 32 NVIDIA A100 GPUs for 15 hours.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">VI-G</span> </span><span id="S6.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open Vocabulary Video Instance Segmentation</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span> We compare the experimental results of all OVVIS methods on the LV-VIS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite> validation and test sets. LV-VIS is a large-vocabulary video instance segmentation dataset comprising 4,828 real-world videos spanning 1,196 categories. The videos are partitioned into 3,083 for training, 837 for validation, and 908 for testing. The categories are divided into 641 base categories—inherited from frequent and common classes in LVIS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib202" title="" class="ltx_ref">202</a>]</cite>—and 555 novel categories that are disjoint from the base categories. We adopt the Average Precision (AP) as the evaluation metric, computed as the mean over multiple IoU thresholds from 0.5 to 0.95. To better assess open-vocabulary performance, we also report APn, which denotes the average AP exclusively on novel categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span>
As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ V-E General Video Understanding ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, Recent OVVIS methods have adopted the paradigm of post-network tuning for image-to-video transfer learning, incorporating CLIP to introduce open-vocabulary capabilities. Through a comparative analysis of several CLIP-based methods, we observe that CLIP-VIS<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite> achieves the best performances. This can be primarily attributed to its introduction of a temporal Top-K enhanced matching strategy. Instead of matching only adjacent frames or all historical frames, this approach selects the top K most similar frames from the past for query association. This mechanism reduces interference from noisy frames and mitigates error propagation, thereby enhancing tracking stability. Furthermore, the method integrates classification scores with an objectness score and a mask IoU score. This fusion strengthens the consistency between classification and segmentation quality, improving robustness against challenges such as occlusion and complex scenes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS8.6" class="ltx_text">VI-H</span> </span><span id="S6.SS8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open Vocabulary Multi-Object Tracking</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Datasets and Metrics.</span> For the OVMOT task, we adopt the TAO<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib203" title="" class="ltx_ref">203</a>]</cite> dataset as benchmark and compare the performance of existing image-to-video transfer methods. The TAO dataset is a comprehensive video collection designed to provide rich visual and semantic annotations for diverse video analysis tasks. A key characteristic of TAO is its open-world nature, enabling the tracking of arbitrary objects rather than restricting to predefined categories. The dataset is partitioned into three subsets: training, validation, and testing, comprising 500, 988, and 1,419 videos respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The OVMOT method is primarily evaluated using the TETA metric. TETA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib204" title="" class="ltx_ref">204</a>]</cite>, an extension of HOTA<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib205" title="" class="ltx_ref">205</a>]</cite>, enhances its functionality to address challenges in long-tail scenarios. The TETA score comprises three components: a localization score (LocA), a classification score (ClsA), and an association score (AssocA). LocA quantifies the precision of bounding box localization, measuring the spatial alignment between predicted and ground-truth boxes, irrespective of classification or association accuracy. ClsA evaluates the correctness of object classification into predefined categories (e.g., ’car’, ’pedestrian’, ’bicycle’), independent of localization or association performance. AssocA assesses the robustness of identity preservation over time, reflecting the consistency of object-ID assignments across frames and directly capturing tracking and data association efficacy. The overall TETA score is computed as the arithmetic mean of these three sub-scores.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS8.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results and analysis.</span> In the OVMOT task, researchers explored multiple image-to-video transfer paradigms, including distillation, adapter modules, and post-network tuning, while also experimenting with two distinct foundation models: CLIP<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> and GroundingDINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. As shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ V-E General Video Understanding ‣ V Coarse-grained Video Understanding ‣ Image-to-Video Transfer Learning based on Image-Language Foundation Models: A Comprehensive Survey" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>, no single method clearly outperforms the others or achieves superior performance across all metrics. Specifically, the CLIP-based GLATrack model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, which performs image-to-video transfer via knowledge distillation, achieves the highest scores on TETA and AssocA, surpassing other competitors by more than 1%. In contrast, the GroundingDINO-based approaches MASA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib107" title="" class="ltx_ref">107</a>]</cite> and NetTrack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite> perform better on LocA and ClsA, respectively, highlighting that different methods excel in different evaluation criteria.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">FUTURE DIRECTIONS AND DISCUSSION</span></h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the challenges and potential research directions related to transfer useful knowledge from pre-trained vision-language foundation model for video-text multi-modality understanding.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Challenges</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A key challenge in transferring vision-language foundation models to the video domain lies in the limited and specialized nature of their capabilities. For instance, CLIP is primarily designed for image-text alignment, BLIP focuses on vision-language pre-training for image captioning and question answering, MDETR emphasizes grounding textual descriptions in object detection tasks, while GroundingDINO targets phrase grounding with strong detection performance. Despite their effectiveness within individual domains, no unified model has yet demonstrated robust and comprehensive capabilities across diverse tasks. As a result, different application scenarios in video generation, understanding, or editing often require selecting distinct foundation models, leading to fragmented solutions. Moreover, a standardized and effective strategy for fine-tuning these models on video tasks has yet to be established. Current research predominantly investigates diverse transfer paradigms in an exploratory fashion, highlighting both the immaturity of existing approaches and the experimental stage of this research direction.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VII-B</span> </span><span id="S7.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Future Directions</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As the transfer of image-language foundation models to video-language tasks continues to evolve, several promising research directions emerge. Below, we outline three key trends that are likely to shape future efforts in this field.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unified Transfer Learning Paradigm for Multiple Tasks.</span>
A significant and promising future direction is the development of a unified transfer learning framework that enables a single ILFM to be effectively adapted for multiple video-language tasks simultaneously. Current methods often require designing distinct fine-tuning strategies or adding task-specific modules for each target task (e.g., action recognition, temporal grounding, video QA), which is inefficient and lacks generalization. Future research should focus on creating a more universal adaptation mechanism. This could involve exploring prompt-based learning, where task-specific instructions guide the model’s behavior, or designing parameter-efficient tuning methods (e.g., unified adapters, hypernetworks) that share most parameters across tasks. The ultimate goal is a single, unified model that can perform a wide array of video understanding tasks through a simple and consistent interface, moving beyond specific solutions toward a more general-purpose video intelligence system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Collaboration of Multiple Foundation Models.</span>
Another promising avenue involves effectively integrating multiple pre-trained foundation models—each specializing in a different modality or capability—to tackle a single video-language task. For example, combining large language models (LLMs) with vision foundation models could enhance contextual reasoning and temporal understanding in complex video tasks. Research efforts could focus on fusion mechanisms, knowledge distillation, and cross-model attention to harness complementary strengths while mitigating computational costs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Advanced Fusion Methods for Dual-Modality Integration.</span>
Despite progress, effectively aligning and fusing visual and linguistic features across spatial and temporal dimensions remains challenging. Future studies may investigate more dynamic and efficient fusion techniques, such as cross-modal transformers, graph-based alignment, or lightweight feature interaction modules. Improving the synergy between video and text modalities will be critical for tasks requiring fine-grained understanding, such as spatio-temporal grounding or long-form video reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These directions highlight the need for more efficient, integrative, and scalable approaches to bridge image-language models and video-language applications, paving the way for richer video understanding systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VII-C</span> </span><span id="S7.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">More Discussion</span></h3>

<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This survey primarily reviews the literature on transferring knowledge from image–text foundation models to advance video–text understanding. Indeed, recent studies have increasingly explored extending these foundation models with image-to-video transfer learning techniques for video editing and generation. A brief overview of related works is provided here, and readers are referred to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib206" title="" class="ltx_ref">206</a>]</cite> for a more comprehensive and systematic survey.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Methods.</span>
Video generation aims to create visually coherent and semantically meaningful video sequences from various inputs, such as text or images. The advent of vision–language foundation models such as Stable Diffusion (SD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite> has stimulated extensive research on video generation, a task that demands effective modeling of temporal coherence and dynamic motion. Early works, like ModelScope Text-to-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">85</a>]</cite>, modified the latent diffusion U-Net by adding 3D convolutions and temporal attention to the frozen spatial attention block, enabling direct text-to-video synthesis in the latent space. At the same time, commercial systems such as Runway Gen-2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib207" title="" class="ltx_ref">207</a>]</cite> began to demonstrate advanced text-to-video capabilities, likely based on optimized spatiotemporal latent diffusion frameworks. A different approach was proposed by AnimateDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib208" title="" class="ltx_ref">208</a>]</cite>, which introduced a lightweight Motion Module trained independently and injected into frozen U-Nets of pre-trained SD checkpoints using Motion LoRA. This design decouples the learning of appearance from that of motion, thereby improving the efficiency of animation. Subsequent works like VideoCrafter1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">83</a>]</cite> further refined spatiotemporal U-Net architectures by partial tuning, often using causal 3D convolutions. For image-to-video generation, I2VGen-XL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a>]</cite> employed a hierarchical latent diffusion framework that upsampled videos progressively across multiple stages. More recently, Stability AI released Stable Video Diffusion (SVD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite>, which extends and fully fine-tunes image-based Stable Diffusion models by incorporating temporal layers. This design enables the generation of short, temporally consistent video clips and even multi-view outputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Text-driven video editing aims to generate high-quality videos aligned with a target prompt while preserving the source video’s spatio-temporal structure. The main challenge is balancing flexible semantic edits with temporal consistency. Significant advancements in video editing stem from leveraging large-scale, pretrained vision-language foundation models, particularly Stable Diffusion (SD)<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a>]</cite>. This marks a departure from traditional video processing pipelines that handled frames individually, often leading to flickering artifacts and quality degradation. By adapting models initially trained on static images, researchers now explicitly address the temporal dimension inherent in video data, aiming for coherent edits across sequences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Pioneering work Tune-A-Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>]</cite> transferred pretrained Stable Diffusion to video tasks through side-tuning spatio-temporal attention modules. It introduced sparse-causal and temporal attention layers within the U-Net to improve consistency, but required costly per-video fine-tuning. To overcome this limitation, Text2Video-Zero <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib209" title="" class="ltx_ref">209</a>]</cite> proposed a training-free approach. It integrated motion dynamics using cross-frame attention and latent warping, though performance dropped on complex motions due to reliance on implicit cues. Subsequent zero-shot methods explored richer conditioning strategies. Runway Gen-1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>]</cite> combined CLIP embeddings and depth maps with a finetuned convolution–attention U-Net. Pix2Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib134" title="" class="ltx_ref">134</a>]</cite> applied DDIM inversion and depth-guided denoising, preserving structure by injecting latent features from earlier frames without training. ControlNet <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib136" title="" class="ltx_ref">136</a>]</cite> was adapted in ControlVideo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib210" title="" class="ltx_ref">210</a>]</cite>, which combined cross-frame attention and interpolation for multimodal structural guidance in a training-free manner. Rerender A Video <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib211" title="" class="ltx_ref">211</a>]</cite> improved temporal smoothness with sparse causal attention, latent warping, and optical-flow–based fusion in both latent and pixel spaces. More recent works emphasize explicit motion and correspondence modeling. TokenFlow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib135" title="" class="ltx_ref">135</a>]</cite> introduced a key-frame–centric global attention strategy. FLATTEN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib212" title="" class="ltx_ref">212</a>]</cite> propagated pixel correspondences via optical flow within a training-free temporal-guided attention. Building on this, FRESCO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">82</a>]</cite> refined flow-based methods with occlusion masks and combined cross-frame flow attention with intra-frame similarity. Together, these developments highlight a shift from implicit motion cues toward explicit modeling, enabling more faithful and temporally coherent video editing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation Datasets and Metrics.</span>
Video generation and editing rely heavily on large-scale datasets and comprehensive evaluation protocols. Commonly used datasets include WebVid <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib213" title="" class="ltx_ref">213</a>]</cite> for text–video pairs, LAION <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib214" title="" class="ltx_ref">214</a>]</cite> for large-scale image–text pairs, Kinetics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite> for human actions, HowTo100M <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib215" title="" class="ltx_ref">215</a>]</cite> for instructional videos, and DAVIS for video editing benchmarks. These datasets provide rich motion, semantic, and structural priors essential for training and evaluation. System performance is typically assessed using objective metrics such as Fréchet Video Distance (FVD) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib216" title="" class="ltx_ref">216</a>]</cite> for temporal consistency and perceptual quality, Fréchet Inception Distance (FID) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib217" title="" class="ltx_ref">217</a>]</cite> and Inception Score (IS) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib218" title="" class="ltx_ref">218</a>]</cite> for fidelity, and CLIP-based measures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib219" title="" class="ltx_ref">219</a>]</cite> for text–video alignment. For editing tasks, frame-wise accuracy (Fram-Acc) and temporal coherence (Tmp-Con) are further employed to evaluate spatial preservation and temporal stability. Beyond these automated measures, human evaluation remains indispensable for judging higher-level aspects such as narrative coherence, creativity, and artifact detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.SS3.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Analysis.</span>
Recent studies have shown that temporal coherence can be enhanced by integrating temporal attention into diffusion backbones such as Stable Diffusion, either through partial fine-tuning or by incorporating information from preceding frames in a training-free manner. Furthermore, leveraging auxiliary modalities such as depth maps and optical flow strengthens temporal modeling and enables finer-grained spatial understanding, thereby improving the quality of both video generation and editing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">However, with the growing availability of open-source video foundation models such as SVD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>]</cite> and WAN <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib220" title="" class="ltx_ref">220</a>]</cite>, recent studies have shifted toward training and fine-tuning video-based foundation models, which have demonstrated superior performance as compared with image-to-video transfer methods built upon image–language foundation models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VIII </span><span id="S8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CONCLUSION</span></h2>

<div id="S8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This survey presents a comprehensive overview of recent advancements in image-to-video transfer learning within multimodal learning, which, to the best of our knowledge, is the first of its kind. We begin by outlining the necessary background, including fundamental concepts and an introduction to common image-language foundation models (ILFMs). Subsequently, we summarize nine distinct transfer paradigms employed for image-to-video transfer learning. For each video task, we categorize existing ILFM-based methods according to their technical perspectives and the paradigms used for transferring knowledge from ILFMs. In the experimental section, we detail the evaluation settings and present a fair comparison of results to analyze the performance of different transfer paradigms across various tasks. Finally, we discuss several prevailing challenges and suggest promising future research directions for image-to-video transfer learning.</p>
</div>
</section>
